# Preserve canonical scope unions in shared OAuth tokens

Use this when a broad Google authorization works immediately but later API calls fail with `insufficient authentication scopes`.

## Root cause to test before reauthorizing

A narrow consumer may load a shared token with only its local scope (for example `gmail.modify` or `youtube.upload`), refresh it, then overwrite the shared token JSON. That can collapse saved scope metadata and cause future refreshes to mint narrowed access tokens even though the original refresh grant remains broad.

## Repair workflow

1. Inventory maintained Google API consumers and build a canonical scope union per credential lane. Keep Workspace, YouTube user OAuth, Cloud service accounts, and downloader/browser credentials separate.
2. Inspect every shared-token writer. Consumers must inherit the token's stored scope set or request the lane's canonical union; they must not persist a local one-feature scope list into the shared token.
3. Before asking the user to authorize again, construct credentials in memory from the existing refresh token with the canonical union and run harmless live probes. If Google can mint the union, repair the token atomically without user interaction.
4. If the refresh grant is genuinely revoked or cannot mint required scopes, generate one fresh profile-specific PKCE URL with `access_type=offline`, `include_granted_scopes=true`, and explicit consent/account selection.
5. During callback exchange, require state/PKCE match, exact required-scope subset, a refresh token, and expected account identity before replacing the existing token. Stage and atomically rename the token; reject incomplete grants without overwriting the working file.
6. Verify more than `credentials.valid`: require scope completeness, refresh-token presence, identity match, and harmless live Gmail/Calendar/Drive/People probes. Docs/Sheets can be checked from granted scopes unless an existing document ID supports a non-destructive read.
7. Delete callback documents and pending state after successful exchange.
8. Replay only jobs whose product-level output was actually blocked by auth; do not replay unrelated source/provider failures.

## Persistence limits

Access tokens expire normally and should auto-refresh. Refresh tokens can be long-lived when offline access is granted and the OAuth consent app is in Production, but Google can still revoke them for user action, security events, inactivity, client deletion/reset, or token-count limits. An OAuth app left in Testing may impose short refresh-token lifetimes. Do not promise indefinite authorization.

## User-facing behavior

Audit all maintained projects before presenting URLs, provide every fresh labeled URL directly in chat, invalidate older pending states, and explain exactly which account to use. Avoid repeated reauthorization when an existing broad refresh grant can be repaired locally.
